Fix the engine so a clock time is read as a time, and so a stop written
straight against a digit does not silence the utterance.

  1. A digit-flanked colon produced no audio at all. The transition table
     gives such a colon a state of its own, so a time arrives at
     number_out() as a single token, "8:00"; that function rejected any
     run containing a non-digit, and what followed fell to a fallback that
     never reached the machine. Each run either side of the colon is now
     read as a number, with nothing said between them -- which is what the
     hyphen path does, and why its output is byte-identical to spelling
     the words out.

  2. A run can arrive with a leading character that is not a digit. A stop
     written straight against a digit -- "Read.6:23 PM", which is how an
     accessibility value often reads -- restarts the token at the stop, so
     the run is [".6"]. It was rejected, and the machine was left on the
     same token, spinning until scan()'s guard expired: no audio at all,
     and millions of repeats of one line if the tokeniser's trace is on.
     A space after the stop hid the fault, because the space is eaten and
     the token restarts on the digit. The leading non-digits are now
     skipped, so the digits after them are read.

  3. The 1998 English module's digit-name, tens and teens table addresses
     were four bytes low, so every digit resolved one slot into the next
     name -- a single digit read the following digit's name, and a
     two-digit run of 10 or less ran off the end of the table. That is the
     "minutes starting with a zero are read as a long sequence" fault.
     The other five 1998 languages copy this map address for address.

Apply from the root of an openbst checkout:
    git apply Patches/openbst-clock-times.patch

diff --git a/src/text.c b/src/text.c
index f8acae3..4f75551 100644
--- a/src/text.c
+++ b/src/text.c
@@ -190,9 +190,9 @@ const bst_tabmap BST_MAP_1998_ENG = {
         [BST_S_ORD_FIRST]= 0x00102400, [BST_S_ORD_TIETH]= 0x00102404,
         [BST_S_ORD_TH]   = 0x00102408, [BST_S_GRPSEP]   = 0x00102410,
         [BST_S_PLURAL]   = 0x00102400,
-        [BST_S_DIGITS]   = 0x00101f76, [BST_S_TENS]     = 0x00101fe0,
-        [BST_S_TEENS]    = 0x00102058, [BST_S_SCALES]   = 0x00102140,
-        [BST_S_ZERO]     = 0x00102036,
+        [BST_S_DIGITS]   = 0x00101f7a, [BST_S_TENS]     = 0x00101fe4,
+        [BST_S_TEENS]    = 0x0010205c, [BST_S_SCALES]   = 0x00102140,
+        [BST_S_ZERO]     = 0x0010203a,
     },
 };
 
diff --git a/src/tokenise.c b/src/tokenise.c
index 32bc8fa..538e13a 100644
--- a/src/tokenise.c
+++ b/src/tokenise.c
@@ -577,8 +577,34 @@ static int number_out(bst_tok *t) {
     while (t->cur >= t->start && !is_digit(t, t->ring[t->cur])) unread(t, 1);
     int n = t->cur - t->start + 1;
     if (n <= 0) return 0;
-    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
-        if (!is_digit(t, t->ring[t->start + i])) return 0;
+
+    /* A run can arrive with a leading character that is not a digit. A stop
+       written straight against a digit -- "Read.6:23 PM" -- restarts the token
+       at the stop, so the run is [".6"]. The stop has already been given its
+       meaning by the punctuation transition; what is left here is the digits.
+       Without this the whole run was rejected and the machine was left on the
+       same token, spinning until its guard expired: the utterance came out with
+       no sound at all, and the same guard filled the log with millions of
+       repeats of one line. A space after the stop did not show the fault
+       because the space is eaten and the token restarts on the digit. */
+    while (t->start <= t->cur && !is_digit(t, t->ring[t->start])) t->start++;
+    n = t->cur - t->start + 1;
+    if (n <= 0) return 0;
+
+    /* A colon between two runs of digits gets a state of its own in the
+       transition table, so a time arrives here as a single token, "8:00". A
+       colon is not a digit, so the check below used to reject the whole run;
+       what followed was left to a fallback that never reached the machine and
+       the utterance came out with no sound at all. The colon is a group
+       separator -- the same mark a hyphen between digits becomes -- so each
+       run either side of it is read and nothing is said between them, which
+       is what the hyphen path does. */
+    int has_colon = 0;
+    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
+        int ch = t->ring[t->start + i];
+        if (ch == ':') { has_colon = 1; continue; }
+        if (!is_digit(t, ch)) return 0;
+    }
     if (t->img->t.num_one_digit && n > 1) return 0;
     /* The Arabic and Japanese builds have the tables for a number and never
        reach them: a run of digits leaves no sound at all. */
@@ -587,6 +613,20 @@ static int number_out(bst_tok *t) {
         t->kind = 6;
         return 1;
     }
+    if (has_colon) {
+        int at = t->start;
+        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
+            if (i == n || t->ring[t->start + i] == ':') {
+                int len = t->start + i - at;
+                if (len > 0) bst_say_number(t, t->ring + at, len);
+                at = t->start + i + 1;
+            }
+        }
+        if (t->money) say_currency(t, t->ring + t->start, n);
+        t->prevkind = t->kind;
+        t->kind = 6;
+        return 1;
+    }
     bst_say_number(t, t->ring + t->start, n);
     if (t->money) say_currency(t, t->ring + t->start, n);
     t->prevkind = t->kind;
